    "question": {
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{3}y=2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{3}y & =2y \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{3}y-2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}+({x}^{3}-2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{\u2032}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{3}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{\u2032}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{3}y=-4x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{\u2032}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=-4x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{3}y=2y$; ${y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{3}y=y$ only.",
